1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:06,700
In this demo, we'll start with the Monitoring Sheet. First, Graduate School staff signs in.

2
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:21,900
Here we have the Monitoring Sheet, under Records. Each row is a student, and each column is a subject. The colours show what is completed, what is enrolled, and what is not taken yet.

3
00:00:22,800 --> 00:00:34,000
Let's start by adding a student who is missing from the imported sheet. The window warns that this is a manual entry, and it records who made it and when.

4
00:00:36,100 --> 00:00:50,299
The form asks for the student ID and the name. The school year the student started comes already filled in. The same checks as an import still apply, so the same student can't be added twice.

5
00:00:50,500 --> 00:00:55,399
And just like that, the student is added, with a Student Portal login.

6
00:00:56,299 --> 00:01:04,099
Next, we have the record panel. It lists all eighteen subjects. None are taken yet, and the units are counted automatically.

7
00:01:05,900 --> 00:01:14,299
Now Graduate School staff adds a subject, marks it Completed, picks the term from the list, and adds a remark.

8
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:25,599
Notice that both new rows say Manual entry in the Source column. That way, hand-typed data is never mixed up with imported data.

9
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:32,299
If a remark needs fixing, it's a simple edit.

10
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:43,700
What about removing a row? That needs a reason. The subject goes back to not taken, and the removal stays in the history.

11
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:59,599
Speaking of history, here we have the Change history tab. It shows every add, change, and removal: the old value, the new value, who did it, when, and why. That is the audit trail.

12
00:02:01,299 --> 00:02:08,700
Back on the grid, the new student now has a Manual tag, and each hand-entered cell shows an orange dot.

13
00:02:09,699 --> 00:02:18,300
Now let's compare that with an imported student. Every row here says Imported, because the values come from the official workbook.

14
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:29,400
Changing an imported value needs a reason, and the staff member's name is saved with it. And grades are never typed in here. AIMS stays the official source.

15
00:02:31,659 --> 00:02:37,659
Next, we have the Leave of Absence. Meet Daniel, a Master of Arts in Education student.

16
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:45,659
Under Requests, Daniel opens Leave of Absence. Right away, the form shows the limit: two semesters at a time.

17
00:02:46,659 --> 00:02:54,459
Here we have a panel that lists every rule the screen follows, each with its page in the Handbook.

18
00:02:57,959 --> 00:03:02,060
Now Daniel picks the dates, chooses a reason, and adds a short explanation.

19
00:03:03,159 --> 00:03:07,359
Before sending, Daniel gets a summary to check.

20
00:03:07,560 --> 00:03:12,060
The request is sent, and now it waits for Graduate School staff.

21
00:03:12,259 --> 00:03:15,659
So let's switch over. Graduate School staff signs in.

22
00:03:17,959 --> 00:03:25,060
Here we have the Leave of Absence board. Each column is a stage, and each card is a request.

23
00:03:26,959 --> 00:03:33,759
Graduate School staff drags the card to In staff review. The server checks every move before it counts.

24
00:03:40,060 --> 00:03:48,259
Next is the policy check. It gives advice, either Pass or Needs review. But it never makes the decision. The Dean does.

25
00:03:52,560 --> 00:03:57,959
So Graduate School staff forwards the request to the Dean.

26
00:03:58,159 --> 00:04:03,959
The card moves to With the Dean, and staff can no longer change it.

27
00:04:04,159 --> 00:04:07,560
Now it's the Dean's turn. The Dean signs in.

28
00:04:15,560 --> 00:04:20,360
The Dean drags the card to Leave scheduled.

29
00:04:20,560 --> 00:04:26,959
And the leave is approved and flagged for the Registrar. The Dean could also have denied it.

30
00:04:27,659 --> 00:04:35,659
Back to the office side. Graduate School staff signs in again.

31
00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:45,060
For this demo, Graduate School staff starts the leave by hand.

32
00:04:45,259 --> 00:04:48,659
Daniel is now on leave, and enrollment is closed.

33
00:04:49,459 --> 00:04:56,560
Next, we have the Registrar list. The portal doesn't send e-mail. Graduate School staff sends it the official way.

34
00:05:02,060 --> 00:05:06,560
Then Graduate School staff marks the list as sent to the Registrar.

35
00:05:07,360 --> 00:05:13,860
Let's check back on Daniel. Daniel signs in one more time.

36
00:05:14,060 --> 00:05:17,460
And Daniel's page now shows the status On leave.

37
00:05:19,959 --> 00:05:28,859
Now let's look at Readmission, for students coming back from a leave. Therese has been on approved leave, and signs in.

38
00:05:29,859 --> 00:05:38,459
Here we have the Readmission page. Enrollment stays closed until the readmission is approved. The leave that is ending is already filled in.

39
00:05:39,359 --> 00:05:46,559
The rule says a returning student must write an intent to enroll. That's on page fifty-three of the Handbook.

40
00:05:49,459 --> 00:06:00,159
So Therese picks the return semester and gives a reason. There's nothing to tick. The system shows what it already checked in the records, and what staff still need to confirm.

41
00:06:03,959 --> 00:06:10,159
The request is sent. It goes to Graduate School staff first, and then to the Dean.

42
00:06:10,359 --> 00:06:14,159
Let's switch to the office. Graduate School staff signs in.

43
00:06:16,459 --> 00:06:23,859
Here we have the Readmission board. It works the same way, with its own columns. Therese's card is in Submitted.

44
00:06:28,159 --> 00:06:34,659
Opening it, the request shows the return semester, the intention, and the leave it's linked to.

45
00:06:34,859 --> 00:06:48,759
Next, we have the return checklist. It follows page forty-five of the Handbook. The system already checked the written intention and the subjects left. The Business Office clearance and the interview are for staff to confirm.

46
00:06:48,959 --> 00:06:56,059
The policy check also flags an early return. Only the Dean can allow that, so it shows Needs review.

47
00:06:59,459 --> 00:07:08,159
When forwarding, Graduate School staff ticks the two office checks. Anything left unticked reaches the Dean as Needs review.

48
00:07:10,259 --> 00:07:14,459
Now it's the Dean's turn. The Dean signs in to decide.

49
00:07:22,559 --> 00:07:27,759
The Dean approves the return and adds a note.

50
00:07:27,959 --> 00:07:36,159
And Therese is active again. Readmission restores the student's standing, but it doesn't enroll any subjects. The Academic Coordinator does that next.

51
00:07:36,859 --> 00:07:44,559
Back to the office side. Graduate School staff signs in again.

52
00:07:44,759 --> 00:07:52,860
On the staff board, the Academic Coordinator is now the next owner, and the case is flagged for the Registrar.

53
00:07:53,059 --> 00:08:00,860
Here we have the same Registrar list as before. Download it, send it, mark it sent, and then mark it acknowledged.

54
00:08:01,559 --> 00:08:06,159
Then Therese signs back in.

55
00:08:06,359 --> 00:08:11,259
Therese can see the approval, along with the messages from staff and the Dean.

56
00:08:13,319 --> 00:08:21,219
Next, we have AWOL, and the return from AWOL. This one starts on the office side. Graduate School staff signs in.

57
00:08:22,120 --> 00:08:32,419
Here we have the AWOL and Residency board. It flags cases straight from the imported enrollment records. Nobody marks a student AWOL by hand.

58
00:08:32,620 --> 00:08:41,919
The card for Maya Torres is in the AWOL column, waiting on the student. Staff can't move it. Only the student can start a return.

59
00:08:42,819 --> 00:08:54,519
Residence limits also apply. A master's student has five years normally, and seven at most. A doctoral student has seven, and nine at most. Time on leave counts too.

60
00:08:55,620 --> 00:09:03,419
The card shows the AWOL date, where the flag came from, and the last semester the student enrolled.

61
00:09:03,620 --> 00:09:07,019
Now let's see the student's side. Maya signs in.

62
00:09:08,019 --> 00:09:15,220
Here we have the Return from AWOL page. It shows the flag. Registration stays restricted until the return is reviewed.

63
00:09:18,620 --> 00:09:28,120
The next open semester is already picked, and the last enrolled semester comes from the records. So Maya only writes the intention and the reason.

64
00:09:30,919 --> 00:09:36,019
The request is sent. The system checks that Maya is still within the maximum residence.

65
00:09:36,720 --> 00:09:44,319
Back to the office side. Graduate School staff signs in again.

66
00:09:44,519 --> 00:09:50,620
On the staff board, the card has moved to Return review on its own.

67
00:09:50,819 --> 00:09:55,620
Opening it, the request shows the intention and the residence limits.

68
00:09:59,419 --> 00:10:05,220
Graduate School staff forwards it to the Dean, with a note.

69
00:10:07,319 --> 00:10:10,720
Now it's the Dean's turn. The Dean signs in.

70
00:10:18,620 --> 00:10:26,419
The Dean drags the card to Back in the program. The approval restores the student's standing.

71
00:10:26,620 --> 00:10:28,519
And Maya is active again.

72
00:10:29,220 --> 00:10:35,720
Graduate School staff signs in one last time.

73
00:10:35,919 --> 00:10:42,019
Next, the Academic Coordinator is the owner, because enrolling subjects is a separate step.

74
00:10:44,819 --> 00:10:53,019
And here we have the Registrar list. It has one report for each approved return. Staff download it and send it officially.

75
00:10:53,919 --> 00:10:58,220
Then Maya signs in again.

76
00:10:58,419 --> 00:11:07,120
Maya can see the approval. So leave, readmission, and AWOL returns all use the same board, the same request window, and the same Registrar list.

77
00:11:09,420 --> 00:11:13,519
Next, we have Reports and Analytics. Graduate School staff signs in.

78
00:11:16,620 --> 00:11:27,720
Here we have Reports and Analytics, under Overview. At the top are the filters: program, year started, and period. Below them are the six numbers the office asks about most.

79
00:11:28,019 --> 00:11:34,519
First, Behind schedule. It's the share of active students who are running late, compared to a target.

80
00:11:34,720 --> 00:11:41,420
Then Median days to decide, compared to the twelve months before. Every number here comes from recorded events.

81
00:11:42,120 --> 00:11:49,920
Next, we have Needs attention. It shows one bar per cause. The longest bar affects the most students.

82
00:11:50,120 --> 00:11:55,720
Clicking a cause narrows the list to just the students it affects.

83
00:11:55,920 --> 00:12:05,620
Show all causes brings the full list back, most delayed first, with the office that can move each student forward. It downloads as Excel or PDF.

84
00:12:06,920 --> 00:12:15,019
Next, we have Where students get stuck. It counts the students at each stage, as on time, slow, or stuck.

85
00:12:15,220 --> 00:12:21,519
Every count is a button. Click it to see the actual students behind the number.

86
00:12:22,920 --> 00:12:31,120
Then Office speed. It shows which offices take longest to close requests. The longest red bar is the office to ask first.

87
00:12:32,420 --> 00:12:42,519
Next, we have Records to fix. It lists research students with missing details, such as no adviser. Once a record is fixed, it drops off the list.

88
00:12:43,820 --> 00:12:48,019
This next tab shows students by year started and by stage.

89
00:12:49,320 --> 00:12:54,220
Then Graduation candidates. It shows who can graduate, and what is still missing.

90
00:12:55,519 --> 00:12:58,920
Overdue and pending lists every waiting task, longest first.

91
00:13:00,320 --> 00:13:06,620
Defenses shows the ones already held and the ones coming up, with the panel and the verdict.

92
00:13:08,019 --> 00:13:13,220
And Registrar changes is the daily list the Registrar needs to apply in AIMS.

93
00:13:16,120 --> 00:13:23,420
One last thing. Every filter applies to the whole page. Pick one program, and all the numbers follow.

94
00:13:25,480 --> 00:13:31,480
Finally, let's go through the five best features of the portal. Graduate School staff signs in.

95
00:13:33,080 --> 00:13:49,580
First, we have rules with a source. Every rule the workflows follow is kept in one register. Each rule shows its page in the Handbook or the Research Protocol, and a label: enforced by the system, documented only, or needs review.

96
00:13:49,779 --> 00:13:55,080
Search for a topic, and the matching rules come up, each with its page.

97
00:14:00,279 --> 00:14:09,779
Second, we have the Policy Assistant. It answers only from the Graduate School's own documents. Ask a question, and the answer names its source.

98
00:14:09,980 --> 00:14:17,480
This site runs without the Gemini key, so this is the offline answer. Clicking the source opens the exact passage.

99
00:14:22,679 --> 00:14:30,279
Third, we have panel matching that explains itself. Each faculty member gets a score out of one hundred.

100
00:14:30,480 --> 00:14:38,679
The breakdown is right on screen: expertise, availability, and workload, with the passage that matched. A person still makes the final choice.

101
00:14:44,580 --> 00:14:56,679
Fourth, we have scheduling that checks before it books. It reads everyone's availability and suggests the best shared times.

102
00:14:56,879 --> 00:15:05,779
Hard stops, like a panelist or a room booked twice, can't be overridden. Warnings, like a short lead time, need a reason on record.

103
00:15:08,379 --> 00:15:17,679
Fifth, we have shared boards. Leave, readmission, AWOL, withdrawal, practicum, and graduation all run on the same kind of board, and every move is recorded.

104
00:15:21,580 --> 00:15:27,679
And Reports and Analytics are built from those records. Nothing is typed in by hand.

105
00:15:27,879 --> 00:15:40,180
To sum up, each feature takes data the Graduate School already has, adds the policy behind it, and turns it into a decision, a warning, or a number to track.

106
00:15:40,379 --> 00:15:52,279
To be honest about the limits: the portal doesn't send e-mail, Google Calendar is optional, official data comes only from AIMS, and rules marked Needs review still wait for validation. Thank you.
